  ],
  "question": {
    "text": "Total numbers of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>-digit numbers that are divisible by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math> and can be formed by using the digits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>5</mn></math> with repetition, is ________",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "16",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>A number is divisible by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math>&nbsp;when it is divisible by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>&nbsp;both.</p><p>The three digit number can be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><munder><mi>a</mi><mo>&#175;</mo></munder><munder><mi>b</mi><mo>&#175;</mo></munder><munder><mn>2</mn><mo>&#175;</mo></munder></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math>&nbsp;can be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></math></p><p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math>&nbsp;then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;can be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></math></p><p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></math>&nbsp;then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;can be<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;</p><p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></math>&nbsp;then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;can be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>)</mo></math></p><p>So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn></math> such cases when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> is at unit&#39;s place</p><p>Similarly, there exist <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn></math> such cases when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math> is at unit&#39;s place.</p><p>Total&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></math>&nbsp;cases.</p><p>Hence this is the required answer.</p></blockquote>",
